{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>You shall pass it through fire, and it shall be purified</b> – A vessel that came into contact with a corpse or with an animal carcass is not purified by fire, for the immersion of the Torah is only in water. Therefore our Rabbis (b. Avodah Zarah 75b) were required to explain that this purification is to purge them (הגעילם) from the forbidden foods that they absorbed through the agency of the gentiles, and this is true without any doubt." "<b>And all that does not come into fire</b> – any item whose use is not by means of fire, such as goblets and bowls and flasks whose use is with cold contents and which did not absorb a forbidden substance, <b>you shall pass through water</b> – immerse them and that suffices; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 31:23). But it is not correct in my eyes, for the expression \"תַּעֲבִירוּ\" does not mean immersion, since it should have said \"תָּבִיאוּ בַמַּיִם\" — that is the language of immersion, as in \"בַּמַּיִם יוּבָא וְטָמֵא עַד הָעֶרֶב וְטָהֵר\" (Leviticus 11:32). Furthermore, even vessels whose use is with cold contents require purging (הכשר) from the forbidden substance within them in addition to this immersion, so how could he not mention their purging as he did for those that come into fire? And when the Sages learned out this immersion, they did not cite this verse for it, but rather expounded (b. Avodah Zarah ibid.): \"אַךְ בְּמֵי נִדָּה יִתְחַטָּא\" — water in which a menstruant immerses. But the meaning of <b>תַּעֲבִירוּ בַמָּיִם</b> is to wash them and scour them well in water until the rust that adhered to them from the forbidden substance used in them is removed, for this is their purging from the forbidden substance. Scripture says: an item whose use is through fire — you shall bring it into fire, in the same manner that it was used while forbidden: if its use is with actual flame, such as iron and copper and also silver and gold, purify it (לבנו) with fire; and if through hot liquid, such as tin and lead, purge it (הגעילו) with hot liquid. And an item that was not used with fire but only with cold contents — you shall rinse it in water until it is scoured and purified. And so the Sages said (ibid.): one rinses them and immerses them and they are pure. And the language of the Sifrei (Sifrei on Mattot 157) is: \"תַּעֲבִירוּ בָאֵשׁ\" — such as knives, because of the coating (כוויית) of the gentiles; \"וְכֹל אֲשֶׁר לֹא יָבֹא בָאֵשׁ\" — such as goblets; \"תַּעֲבִירוּ בַמַּיִם\" — because of the absorbed flavor of the gentiles (געול הגויים). And Scripture had no need to reiterate the immersion that it had already mentioned — \"אַךְ בְּמֵי נִדָּה יִתְחַטָּא\" — for once the forbidden substance has been purged, they are all equal in the law of immersion. And my heart further ponders saying that this immersion is from rabbinic law (מדבריהם), and the verse is a mere scriptural support (אסמכתא), and thus Onkelos (Onkelos on Numbers 31:23) translates it with reference to the purificatory sprinkling (חיטוי) of the ashes of the red heifer. And the Sages required it only for metal vessels, because among them there are vessels whose use is with fire, and with a first vessel (כלי ראשון), and with a second vessel (כלי שני), and with cold contents — and this requires derivation. And He warned them now about purging the vessels of Midian from the forbidden substances of the gentiles, but He did not say this to them initially regarding the vessels of Sihon and Og, whose spoil they also took, as it says: \"רַק הַבְּהֵמָה בָּזַזְנוּ לָנוּ וּשְׁלַל הֶעָרִים אֲשֶׁר לָכְדְנוּ\" (Deuteronomy 2:35). The reason is that Sihon and Og are kings of the Amorites and their land is part of the inheritance of Israel, and all their spoil was permitted to them, even the forbidden items, as it is written: \"וּבָתִּים מְלֵאִים כָּל טוּב אֲשֶׁר לֹא מִלֵּאתָ\" (ibid. 6:11), and our Rabbis said (b. Chullin 17a): pots full of pork were permitted to them (קדלי דחזירי אשתרי להו). But Midian was not theirs and they did not take their land; only to take vengeance did they kill them and take their spoil, and therefore the prohibition applied to their vessels. And likewise regarding the law of impurity about which He warned them now: \"וְאַתֶּם חֲנוּ מִחוּץ לַמַּחֲנֶה שִׁבְעַת יָמִים\" etc. (Numbers 31:19), for in the war against Sihon and Og all Israel participated and impurity was permitted for the congregation (ציבור). And according to the plain sense (דרך הפשט), He warned them: \"And you, encamp outside the camp seven days and purify yourselves,\" so that they would not render the people impure; but there, they were all equal in the matter."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {1 "The Sifrei citation 'מטות נ' in the source is likely a section/paragraph number rather than a chapter-verse address; rendered here as 'Sifrei on Mattot 157' following common citation conventions for this midrashic unit."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:10:17.331789958Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}